parts:
- text: "I’ll inspect the training interface and baselines, then submit a parsimonious DSConv + power-mean pooling model with sequence-position dropout and stochastic reverse augmentation.\n\n`/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`\n`/execute_action{storage read system/train_single.py}`\n`/execute_action{review 1}`\n`/execute_action{review 876}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv-PowerMean with Position Dropout and Stochastic Reverse Augmentation\"\ntags: \"conv, depthwise-separable, pooling, augmentation, dropout, optimizer-adamw\"\nabstract: \"A compact depthwise-separable conv stack with learnable power-mean pooling, sequence-position dropout, and stochastic reverse augmentation. The architecture is dataset-agnostic with a small MLP head. Optimizer is AdamW with moderate weight decay. Designed for parsimony and robust generalization across heterogeneous RNA tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from flax import linen as nn\n\
    \  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\n  # -------------------------------\n  # Hyperparameters\n  # -------------------------------\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Parsimonious defaults; can be tuned later.\n      return {\n          \"hidden_dim\": 192,\n          \"num_blocks\": 4,\n          \"kernel_size\": 9,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,            # feature dropout inside blocks\n          \"pos_dropout_rate\": 0.05,        # dropout across sequence positions\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"learning_rate\": 1e-3,\n          \"weight_decay\": 0.012,\n          \"powermean_init\": 2.0,           # initial p for power-mean pooling\n          \"enable_reverse_aug\": True,      # stochastic reverse augmentation during training\n      }\n\n  # Optional constants for reproducibility and speed\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  # -------------------------------\n  # Modules\n  # -------------------------------\n  class PositionDropout(nn.Module):\n\
    \      rate: float = 0.0\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool, rng: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          # Broadcast dropout across channels -> drop entire positions consistently across features\n          if self.rate <= 0.0:\n              return x\n          do = nn.Dropout(rate=self.rate, broadcast_dims=(2,))\n          # Flax dropout draws from 'dropout' rng stream; we pass rng via rngs in apply\n          return do(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n  class PowerMeanPooling(nn.Module):\n      axis: int = 1\n      eps: float = 1e-6\n      init_p: float = 2.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # Learnable p >= 1 via softplus + 1 transform\n          # Inverse-softplus init so that softplus(logit_p) + 1 ~= init_p\n          init_logit = jnp.log(jnp.exp(self.init_p - 1.0) - 1.0)\n          logit_p = self.param(\"logit_p\", lambda rng: jnp.array(init_logit))\n          p = nn.softplus(logit_p) + 1.0\n          # Use abs to handle signed activations;\
    \ stabilizes gradients\n          x_abs = jnp.abs(x) + self.eps\n          mean_pow = jnp.mean(x_abs ** p, axis=self.axis)\n          return mean_pow ** (1.0 / p)\n\n  class DSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool):\n          # Pre-norm\n          y = nn.LayerNorm(axis=-1)(x)\n          # Depthwise conv (groups = channels)\n          y = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.features,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              padding=\"SAME\",\n              feature_group_count=self.features,\n              use_bias=False,\n          )(y)\n          y = nn.gelu(y)\n          # Pointwise conv for channel mixing\n          y = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.features,\n              kernel_size=(1,),\n              use_bias=True,\n          )(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n   \
    \       return x + y\n\n  class DSConvBackbone(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      num_blocks: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      pos_dropout_rate: float\n      powermean_init: float\n      enable_reverse_aug: bool\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          # Stochastic reverse augmentation (sequence reversal) during training\n          if (not deterministic) and self.enable_reverse_aug and (rng_key is not None):\n              # With p=0.5, flip along sequence length axis\n              flip_mask = jax.random.bernoulli(rng_key, 0.5, (x.shape[0],))\n              def maybe_flip(seq, do_flip):\n                  return jnp.where(do_flip, jnp.flip(seq, axis=0), seq)\n              # vmap over batch to avoid control flow\n              x = jax.vmap(lambda xi, f: jnp.flip(xi, axis=0) if f else xi, in_axes=(0, 0))(x, flip_mask)\n\n          # Embed channels\
    \ from 4 -> hidden_dim\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(1,), use_bias=True)(x)\n\n          # Stack DSConv blocks\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvBlock(self.hidden_dim, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Position dropout (drop entire positions consistently across channels)\n          h = PositionDropout(self.pos_dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global pooling: concat(mean, max, power-mean(p))\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          max_pool = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n          pmean_pool = PowerMeanPooling(axis=1, init_p=self.powermean_init)(h)\n          pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, max_pool, pmean_pool], axis=-1)\n          return pooled  # shape: (B, 3 * hidden_dim)\n\n  class Head(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      hidden: int\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic:\
    \ bool):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm(axis=-1)(x)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden)(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(h)\n          return logits\n\n  class DSConvPMNet(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n      d_output: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          backbone = DSConvBackbone(\n              hidden_dim=self.hparams[\"hidden_dim\"],\n              num_blocks=self.hparams[\"num_blocks\"],\n              kernel_size=self.hparams[\"kernel_size\"],\n              dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n              pos_dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"pos_dropout_rate\"],\n              powermean_init=self.hparams[\"powermean_init\"],\n              enable_reverse_aug=self.hparams[\"enable_reverse_aug\"],\n          )\n          feats = backbone(x, deterministic=deterministic,\
    \ rng_key=rng_key)\n          head = Head(d_output=self.d_output, hidden=self.hparams[\"head_hidden\"], dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"dropout_rate\"])\n          out = head(feats, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return out\n\n  # Wrapper to satisfy the interface required by the training system\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model: nn.Module, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.model = model\n          self.hparams = hparams\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          # dummy_input: (B, L, 4)\n          variables = self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True, rng_key=None)\n          return variables\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic: bool = True, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          rngs = {\"dropout\": rng_key} if (rng_key is not None and not deterministic) else None\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rng_key=rng_key, rngs=rngs)\n\n  # -------------------------------\n\
    \  # Factory functions\n  # -------------------------------\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"\n      Returns a NetworkWrapper with .init and .apply methods.\n      The training system supplies:\n        - hparams['dataset'], hparams['d_output'], etc.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output = hparams[\"d_output\"]\n      model = DSConvPMNet(hparams=hparams, d_output=d_output)\n      return NetworkWrapper(model, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3, weight_decay: float = 0.012):\n      # AdamW with modest weight decay; b1/b2 defaults are robust\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=weight_decay)\n\n  # -------------------------------\n  # Optional training completion hook\n  # -------------------------------\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      # Minimal: print a compact summary for lineage storage logs\n      ds = trial_data.get(\"dataset\", \"unknown\")\n      val = trial_data.get(\"val_metric\"\
    , None)\n      seed = trial_data.get(\"seed\", None)\n      hp = trial_data.get(\"hyperparameters\", {})\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} | val_metric={val} | seed={seed} | hidden={hp.get('hidden_dim')} blocks={hp.get('num_blocks')} k={hp.get('kernel_size')}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering architecture design**\n\nTo be cautious, I think we should base our design on specific requirements, ensuring the system can handle dataset-specific outputs. I might create an architecture class tailored around this. Also, factoring in training time is key: 30 minutes per dataset, with 100 epochs, means I need to keep the model small for quicker training. Implementing an optimizer like AdamW is also important, possibly incorporating gradient clipping, and I'd like to explore minimal robust baseline architectures. I'll check the framework details to see what fits!**Planning next actions**\n\nFor my next steps, I'll execute certain actions: reading system defaults and a training script. I might also review previous records to see if I can reuse any earlier code within my lineage. I need to be cautious with code reuse, as it’s allowed between agents in the same lineage, not between different ones. The guidelines indicate I can access Noema II's earlier submissions,\
  \ but I should strive for novelty and propose an alternative rather than just replicating the current state-of-the-art. First, checking the default trainer for Hparams is crucial!**Designing the architecture**\n\nI plan to implement a 'create_network' function using our minimal architecture, which emphasizes concepts like reverse invariance, power-mean pooling, and stochastic position dropout from memory capsule 24. I need to confirm the details there by reviewing it, but I haven't opened it yet. For now, I'll access 'review 1' and 'review 876' for code. I can also rank authors in my lineage, although it looks like everything is attributed to Noema II. Then, I’ll focus on crafting the initial baseline method! I'm wondering about reverse invariance in RNA sequences too.**Focusing on RNA tasks**\n\nIn considering RNA tasks, orientation seems crucial. The concept of \"reverse-invariance\" from earlier notes suggests they introduced augmentation or parameter tying, but I want to keep things\
  \ straightforward for our initial submission. I could propose a simple method: Depthwise-Separable ConvNet with Global Power-Mean Pooling and channel-mixers with dropout on positions, plus head dropout for output calibration. I need to ensure that my network's 'init' and 'apply' functions align with the expected API, probably sticking to Flax linen and checking defaults.py for guidance. I'll read the relevant files next!**Planning architecture implementation**\n\nI need to check 'system/defaults.py' as indicated before. After that, I'll list Noema code and proceed to implement the new architecture 'DSConvPM'. I'm focusing on key aspects like dataset-specific outputs, including multiclass and regression tasks. The initial submission should stay moderate without too much complexity. I'll ensure the architecture uses JAX and Flax, carefully defining the 'init' and 'apply' functions and integrating necessary components like PowerMean pooling and dropout layers. There's a lot to consider, especially\
  \ regarding output shapes and loss functions. I’ll need to follow up on gradient clipping and training speed as well!**Implementing DSConv architecture**\n\nI want to check 'train_single.py' to ensure the shapes are correct before moving forward. I'll be writing 'submit 1' with YAML content code for 'submission.py' based on our earlier work. It's essential to keep everything in 'content' since the submission can’t rely on external storage, even though we can import modules. We need to focus on creating the architecture while referencing best scores, aiming for feasible targets. I’ll outline the necessary code for dropout, pooling, and the network setup, including aspects like parameter initialization and function definitions. We should plan for clean organization!**Crafting submission for DSConv**\n\nTo keep parameter sizes manageable, I think concatenating mean, max, and powmean is a good approach — this way, I can enhance feature dimensions while feeding them into 'nn.Dense(d_output).\
  \ I note that using features from both mean and max stacked could simplify the architecture. I'll set up hyperparameters like dropout rates and learning rates, then incorporate the optimizer. \n\nBefore finalizing everything, it’s essential to read through 'defaults.py' and 'train_single.py' to avoid mistakes. I’ll execute necessary commands to access those codes and ensure alignment!**Finalizing test function and actions**\n\nI'm considering adding a 'test()' function to handle scoring without running the complete training. However, it seems I haven't defined it yet. I need to craft an assistant message to include actions like reading 'defaults', 'train_single', and reviewing necessary items. \n\nTo save tokens, I might skip some reads but still check defaults carefully. I’ll also ensure I include the necessary imports before generating the code, considering all edge cases like weighted parameters and ensuring proper return shapes. Let's get coding!"
