parts:
- text: "Checking the running evaluation and preparing a corrected reusable module for future submissions.\n\n`/execute_action{review 921}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/models/dsconv_pm.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from flax import linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\n  class PositionDropout(nn.Module):\n      rate: float = 0.0\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool):\n          if self.rate <= 0.0:\n              return x\n          # Drop entire positions consistently across channels\n          return nn.Dropout(rate=self.rate, broadcast_dims=(2,))(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n  class PowerMeanPooling(nn.Module):\n      axis: int = 1\n      eps: float = 1e-6\n      init_p: float = 2.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # learnable p >= 1 via softplus + 1 transform\n          init_logit = jnp.log(jnp.expm1(self.init_p - 1.0) + 1e-6)\n \
    \         logit_p = self.param(\"logit_p\", lambda rng: jnp.array(init_logit))\n          p = nn.softplus(logit_p) + 1.0\n          x_abs = jnp.abs(x) + self.eps\n          mean_pow = jnp.mean(x_abs ** p, axis=self.axis)\n          return mean_pow ** (1.0 / p)\n\n  class DSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm(axis=-1)(x)\n          c = h.shape[-1]\n          # Depthwise conv: groups = in_channels, multiplier = 1\n          h = nn.Conv(features=c, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding=\"SAME\",\n                      feature_group_count=c, use_bias=False)(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=c, kernel_size=(1,), use_bias=True)(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvBackbone(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n\
    \      num_blocks: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      pos_dropout_rate: float\n      powermean_init: float\n      enable_reverse_aug: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          # Stochastic reverse augmentation (safe JAX form)\n          if (not deterministic) and self.enable_reverse_aug and (rng_key is not None):\n              mask = jax.random.bernoulli(rng_key, 0.5, (x.shape[0], 1, 1))\n              x = jnp.where(mask, jnp.flip(x, axis=1), x)\n\n          # Embed 4 -> hidden_dim\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(1,), use_bias=True)(x)\n\n          # DSConv residual stack\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvBlock(kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(\n                  h, deterministic=deterministic\n              )\n\n          # Position dropout (drop\
    \ whole positions across channels)\n          h = PositionDropout(self.pos_dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global pooling: concat(mean, max, power-mean)\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          max_pool = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n          pmean_pool = PowerMeanPooling(axis=1, init_p=self.powermean_init)(h)\n          return jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, max_pool, pmean_pool], axis=-1)\n\n  class Head(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      hidden: int\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm(axis=-1)(x)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden)(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(h)\n\n  class DSConvPMNet(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n      d_output: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool, rng_key:\
    \ Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          feats = DSConvBackbone(\n              hidden_dim=self.hparams[\"hidden_dim\"],\n              num_blocks=self.hparams[\"num_blocks\"],\n              kernel_size=self.hparams[\"kernel_size\"],\n              dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n              pos_dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"pos_dropout_rate\"],\n              powermean_init=self.hparams[\"powermean_init\"],\n              enable_reverse_aug=self.hparams.get(\"enable_reverse_aug\", False),\n          )(x, deterministic=deterministic, rng_key=rng_key)\n          return Head(\n              d_output=self.d_output,\n              hidden=self.hparams[\"head_hidden\"],\n              dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n          )(feats, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n  class Network:\n      def __init__(self, model: nn.Module, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.model = model\n          self.hparams = hparams\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n\
    \          k1, k2 = jax.random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init({'params': k1, 'dropout': k2}, dummy_input,\n                                      deterministic=True, rng_key=None)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic: bool = True, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if (rng_key is not None and not deterministic) else None\n          return self.model.apply({'params': params}, x,\n                                  deterministic=deterministic, rng_key=rng_key, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams[\"d_output\"]\n      model = DSConvPMNet(hparams=hparams, d_output=d_output)\n      return Network(model, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3, weight_decay: float = 0.012,\n                       clip: float = 0.75, b1: float = 0.9, b2: float = 0.999):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(clip),\n\
    \          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, b1=b1, b2=b2, weight_decay=weight_decay)\n      )\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_pm_v2.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/models')\n  from dsconv_pm import create_network as build_network, create_optimizer as build_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\": 1e-3,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 192,\n          \"num_blocks\": 4,\n          \"kernel_size\": 9,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"pos_dropout_rate\": 0.05,\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"powermean_init\": 2.0,\n          \"enable_reverse_aug\": False\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return build_network(hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3):\n      # Add grad clipping for stability\n      return build_optimizer(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.012,\
    \ clip=0.75)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      print(f\"[Complete] {trial_data.get('dataset')} val_metric={trial_data.get('val_metric')} seed={trial_data.get('seed')}\")\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reviewing model adjustments**\n\nI’m preparing a series of ablation submissions, considering factors like removing power-mean pooling and disabling certain augmentations, which may have a cooldown on submissions — so I can only submit once ready.\n\nI need to review the Noema II private memory for insights, noting that rooms are locked until a certain maturity level. \n\nI might read 'system/main.py' again for training interface usage and also look into defining 'create_optimizer' to include gradient clipping since it's currently not utilized.**Examining optimizer details**\n\nThe default `create_optimizer` uses AdamW without any clipping, and the current submission follows this pattern. While it might be fine, I suspect that adding clipping (around 0.5-0.75) could improve performance in the SOTA region.\n\nI notice we have a `weight_decay` parameter set to 0.012, but I need to confirm if it’s passed in the training system. It looks like the signature in `create_optimizer`\
  \ includes `weight_decay`, but the system calls it with just the learning rate. To implement clipping, I should propose revising this for the next submission.**Updating submission insights**\n\nOur submission is already submitted, but I can still update it by writing new code to the storage.\n\nThe `apply` method uses random number generators (rngs) with `{'dropout': rng_key}` for non-deterministic cases. We need `rng_key` for the flip augmentation in the DSConvBackbone. \n\nI included sequence reversal augmentation with a probability of 0.5, but I'm wondering if this is appropriate for RNA tasks due to directionality. Since reversing could negatively affect certain tasks, I might think about disabling or gating it based on the dataset to keep within the guidelines of not using dataset-specific methods.**Clarifying architecture rules**\n\nThe rule states that the architecture must remain the same, but final layers can be customized. While training heuristics can be generalized, they might\
  \ need to be dataset-specific if we branch out. For compliance, I should avoid dataset-specific toggles and keep things general.\n\nNow, about the MRL dataset: the maximum length should be 59 according to the spec, yet the default `train_single` uses this max length. However, previous baseline evaluation logs indicate \"seq_len=100\" in earlier training, which is confusing compared to the current setup.**Identifying potential issues**\n\nIn `system/train_single`, I see the MRL dataset specifies a `max_seq_len` of 59, but the data might have variable lengths. For one-hot encoding, we need to ensure consistency across sequences. The earlier baseline logs show an inconsistent length of 100, which might correspond to an older version of the script.\n\nAnother concern arises in the DSConvBlock: depthwise convolution's `feature_group_count` uses input channels, but we need to ensure it divides evenly. The need for proper handling of depthwise convolution and its parameters is essential.\n\n\
  Additionally, there’s a possible bug with reverse augmentation due to improper conditionals in the `jax.vmap` function, implying a risk of errors during execution. We should fix this before training starts, even if we can’t change the current submission. I'll prepare the corrected code for later use while choosing to add a `clip_by_global_norm` parameter as well to align with SOTA benchmarks. \n\nFinally, I realize that the `init` method for the network should return parameters directly. It seems our wrapper doesn't align with this, which could lead to optimizer initialization issues later. It’s crucial to resolve these discrepancies to maintain consistent functionality and prevent downstream errors.**Analyzing network initialization and dropout**\n\nI’ve identified a potential issue with how `self.model.init` returns variables, specifically wrapped in a FrozenDict. If we don’t correctly handle the parameters format, it might lead to shape mismatches during optimizer initialization. We\
  \ ensure the `params` returned from `network.init` are properly formatted to align with expectations during the apply call.\n\nAdditionally, I have concerns regarding the implementation of `PositionDropout` which uses `nn.Dropout` with specific dimensions that create a mask shared across channels. This contravenes the expected behavior of dropping entire positions independently.\n\nMoreover, our network outputs look good for classification tasks, and the previous best scores indicate solid results. However, the bug related to reversal augmentation could lead to unexpected crashes. While we wait for the evaluation results, I plan to fix these issues and consider including gradient clipping to improve performance. Writing to storage to maintain progress is a good move.**Planning next steps and fixes**\n\nI’m considering the next steps after reading the instructions. First, I'll execute `/execute_action{review 921}` to check for results when completed. I'll also write a corrected version\
  \ of the DSConv-PM model to store with fixes to flipping using `jnp.where` and `jax.random.bernoulli`. \n\nAdditionally, I’ll define the optimizer with gradient clipping. I want to provide options for dropout and plan to prepare a second submission referencing this module, while keeping reverse augmentation optional. \n\nI'll also create a `test()` function for shape checking but won't include it in the submission. Finally, I’ll ensure consistency with the parameters returned in the wrapper for training.**Action planning**\n\nMy current plan includes a few important steps. First, I’ll execute `'/execute_action{review 921}'` to check progress logs. Next, I'll write the corrected code module `dsconv_pm.py` to storage, followed by creating a wrapper in `dsconv_pm_v2.py` that references `create_network`. \n\nI need to ensure minimal code to avoid excessive token use while maintaining proper YAML quoting and indentation. I’ll implement various classes for dropout and pooling, adjusting specifications\
  \ in the model as outlined. Finally, I plan to submit once cooldown restrictions are clarified.**Writing and preparing code**\n\nI'll finalize the `dsconv_pm.py` code, incorporating necessary classes and functions like `PositionDropout`, `PowerMeanPooling`, and `DSConvBlock`. I ensure the code is clean and minimal. For pooling methods, I’m computing `p` based on `logit_p`, maintaining stability in the implementation.\n\nAfter writing `dsconv_pm.py`, I’ll also create the wrapper in `dsconv_pm_v2.py`. It's crucial to check the cooldown before submitting again, but let's set up the writes and handle everything necessary first. I’ll focus on concise YAML formatting.**Preparing YAML for execution**\n\nI need to be cautious with quoting code in YAML, especially when using special characters over multiple lines. I'll make sure that when I import `optax` for the `create_optimizer`, everything is set correctly. \n\nI plan on presenting minimal text along with my commands, ensuring that each execution\
  \ action is clearly defined on its own line. I might run `/execute_action{rank author}` to list my submissions first, though it isn't strictly necessary. Let's just focus on executing these commands efficiently!"
