role: model
parts:
- text: "Great—922 gives us a solid, functioning DSConv baseline at 0.5965 average. The biggest gaps vs. SOTA are Modif AUC-ROC and MRL/APA, suggesting we need better long-range pattern capture and robust sequence invariance. Next, I’ll add evaluation-time bi-directional averaging (reverse+forward) and enable reverse augmentation during training, plus mild residual scaling for stability.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/models/dsconv_pm.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from flax import linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\n  class PositionDropout(nn.Module):\n      rate: float = 0.0\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool):\n          if self.rate <= 0.0:\n              return x\n          # Drop entire positions consistently across channels\n          return nn.Dropout(rate=self.rate, broadcast_dims=(2,))(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n  class PowerMeanPooling(nn.Module):\n\
    \      axis: int = 1\n      eps: float = 1e-6\n      init_p: float = 2.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # learnable p >= 1 via softplus + 1 transform\n          init_logit = jnp.log(jnp.expm1(self.init_p - 1.0) + 1e-6)\n          logit_p = self.param(\"logit_p\", lambda rng: jnp.array(init_logit))\n          p = nn.softplus(logit_p) + 1.0\n          x_abs = jnp.abs(x) + self.eps\n          mean_pow = jnp.mean(x_abs ** p, axis=self.axis)\n          return mean_pow ** (1.0 / p)\n\n  class DSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      residual_scale: float = 1.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)  # default axis=-1 for compatibility\n          c = h.shape[-1]\n          # Depthwise conv: groups = in_channels, multiplier = 1\n          h = nn.Conv(features=c, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding=\"SAME\",\n                      feature_group_count=c,\
    \ use_bias=False)(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          # Pointwise conv\n          h = nn.Conv(features=c, kernel_size=(1,), use_bias=True)(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + self.residual_scale * h\n\n  class DSConvBackbone(nn.Module):\n      hidden_dim: int\n      num_blocks: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      pos_dropout_rate: float\n      powermean_init: float\n      residual_scale: float = 1.0\n      enable_reverse_aug: bool = False\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          # x: (B, L, 4)\n          # Stochastic reverse augmentation during training\n          if (not deterministic) and self.enable_reverse_aug and (rng_key is not None):\n              mask = jax.random.bernoulli(rng_key, 0.5, (x.shape[0], 1, 1))\n              x = jnp.where(mask, jnp.flip(x, axis=1), x)\n\n          # Embed 4 -> hidden_dim\n\
    \          h = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(1,), use_bias=True)(x)\n\n          # DSConv residual stack\n          for _ in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvBlock(kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                              dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                              residual_scale=self.residual_scale)(\n                  h, deterministic=deterministic\n              )\n\n          # Position dropout (drop whole positions across channels)\n          h = PositionDropout(self.pos_dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global pooling: concat(mean, max, power-mean)\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          max_pool = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n          pmean_pool = PowerMeanPooling(axis=1, init_p=self.powermean_init)(h)\n          return jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, max_pool, pmean_pool], axis=-1)\n\n  class Head(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      hidden: int\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.10\n\n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden)(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(h)\n\n  class DSConvPMNet(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n      d_output: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, *, deterministic: bool, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          # Instantiate shared modules (reuse params across forward and reverse)\n          backbone = DSConvBackbone(\n              hidden_dim=self.hparams[\"hidden_dim\"],\n              num_blocks=self.hparams[\"num_blocks\"],\n              kernel_size=self.hparams[\"kernel_size\"],\n              dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n              pos_dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"pos_dropout_rate\"],\n              powermean_init=self.hparams[\"powermean_init\"],\n              residual_scale=self.hparams.get(\"\
    residual_scale\", 1.0),\n              enable_reverse_aug=self.hparams.get(\"enable_reverse_aug\", False),\n          )\n          head = Head(\n              d_output=self.d_output,\n              hidden=self.hparams[\"head_hidden\"],\n              dropout_rate=self.hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n          )\n\n          eval_bireverse = bool(self.hparams.get(\"eval_bireverse\", False))\n          if deterministic and eval_bireverse:\n              feats_fwd = backbone(x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None)\n              feats_rev = backbone(jnp.flip(x, axis=1), deterministic=True, rng_key=None)\n              logits_fwd = head(feats_fwd, deterministic=True)\n              logits_rev = head(feats_rev, deterministic=True)\n              logits = 0.5 * (logits_fwd + logits_rev)\n          else:\n              feats = backbone(x, deterministic=deterministic, rng_key=rng_key)\n              logits = head(feats, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Shape handling\n          task_type\
    \ = self.hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n          if task_type == \"regression\":\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)  # (batch,)\n          else:\n              return logits  # (batch, d_output)\n\n  class Network:\n      def __init__(self, model: nn.Module, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.model = model\n          self.hparams = hparams\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          k1, k2 = jax.random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init({'params': k1, 'dropout': k2}, dummy_input,\n                                      deterministic=True, rng_key=None)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic: bool = True, rng_key: Optional[jax.Array] = None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if (rng_key is not None and not deterministic) else None\n          return self.model.apply({'params': params}, x,\n                                  deterministic=deterministic, rng_key=rng_key, rngs=rngs)\n\
    \n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams[\"d_output\"]\n      model = DSConvPMNet(hparams=hparams, d_output=d_output)\n      return Network(model, hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3, weight_decay: float = 0.012,\n                       clip: float = 0.75, b1: float = 0.9, b2: float = 0.999):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(clip),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, b1=b1, b2=b2, weight_decay=weight_decay)\n      )\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_pm_v3.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/models')\n\n  from dsconv_pm import create_network as create_network\n  from dsconv_pm import create_optimizer as _opt\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Enable training-time reverse augmentation and eval-time bi-reverse averaging.\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\"\
    : 1e-3,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 224,\n          \"num_blocks\": 6,\n          \"kernel_size\": 9,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"pos_dropout_rate\": 0.07,\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"powermean_init\": 2.0,\n          \"enable_reverse_aug\": True,\n          \"eval_bireverse\": True,\n          \"residual_scale\": 0.5,\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3):\n      # Slightly lower momentum and higher b2 to stabilize with reverse aug\n      return _opt(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.012, clip=0.75, b1=0.85, b2=0.99)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get(\"dataset\")\n      val = trial_data.get(\"val_metric\")\n      seed = trial_data.get(\"seed\")\n      print(f\"[Complete] {ds} val_metric={val} seed={seed}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv-PowerMean v3 — Reverse-Aug + Bi-Reverse Eval, α=0.5, AdamW(0.85,0.99), clip=0.75\"\ntags: \"conv, dsconv,\
    \ pooling, reverse-invariance, regularization, optimizer-adamw, gradient-clipping\"\nabstract: \"Adds training-time reverse augmentation and evaluation-time bi-directional averaging to a compact DSConv + power-mean model, with mild residual scaling (α=0.5) for stability and AdamW(b1=0.85,b2=0.99, clip=0.75). Architecture remains dataset-agnostic.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_pm_v3 import _define_hyperparameters, create_optimizer, complete\n  from dsconv_pm_v3 import create_network as create_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing performance metrics**\n\nI see we have submission 922, which succeeded but has a low score of 0.5965 compared to the state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 0.6914. Next, I’ll dive into the per-dataset metrics to find weaknesses, like the low Modif AUC-ROC of 0.7397 versus the 0.9511 SOTA. I'll consider tuning the architecture or using different pooling methods. It’s all about finding ways to improve the DSConv baseline while keeping it simple and insightful. Let's design some targeted ablations.**Keeping DSConv simple**\n\nI'm thinking about how to maintain simplicity in DSConv. One idea is to introduce reverse-invariance at inference by averaging predictions with a reversed sequence. I see that we already have stochastic reverse augmentation in training turned off in dsconv_pm_v2. I should confirm that the hyperparameter \"enable_reverse_aug\" is set to False, which means we've indeed disabled this feature. It's important to balance complexity with functionality\
  \ while ensuring performance improvements.**Exploring potential improvements**\n\nI'm considering that enabling might enhance invariance, but I see that our training code involves some complexities with rng_key and dropout_key. During training, the train_step passes the dropout key, and we feed the rng_key to our model call. It seems that helpful parameters might be in use, but I wonder about their necessity. \n\nOn another note, I think about using channel-wise z-normalization from RegCal. While I can’t replicate that directly, maybe I could add a scale calibration head, though reusing their methods could complicate things.**Evaluating optimizations**\n\nI’m leaning towards optimization but have some reservations. The training system pads with zeros, which our DSConv may be using. I’m noticing that \"SAME\" convolution can lead to zero-padded positions, and position dropout might contribute to this.\n\nNext, I can optimize hyperparameters such as hidden_dim, considering 192 versus a stronger\
  \ 256. I might also adjust the number of blocks from 4 to 6 and think about dilation. Adding a dilation schedule could help extend the receptive field, so I'll implement a dilated depthwise convolution with a simple dilation pattern of [1, 2, 4, 8].**Planning DSConv ablations**\n\nI think introducing dilations could significantly enhance performance for long sequences, especially for tasks like ncRNA. Using gated linear units (GLU) might improve our model's capacity while maintaining a small parameter count, and it could help to implement channel dropout and monitor dropout rates. \n\nI also want to explore pooling methods and temperature scaling, but we need to ensure any changes remain dataset-agnostic. \n\nFor ablations, I plan steps like implementing dilated convolutions, evaluating reverse augmentation, and refining our pooling strategies while keeping everything manageable time-wise. I’ll work on creating detailed ablation submissions incrementally to ensure our progress remains\
  \ trackable.**Implementing DSConv modifications**\n\nI'm focused on enhancing the DSConvBlock by adding dilation and adjusting the parameters for convolutions using Flax's capabilities. I want to ensure that the reverse averaging during inference is well-implemented. \n\nBy adding features like residual scaling and potential GLU for pointwise convolutions, I’m looking to streamline our model while minimizing risk. I'm also planning to implement a dilation schedule and enabling evaluation-time bi-reversal, hoping it will improve performance across tasks.\n\nFinally, I’ll create a new submission, keeping track of all adjustments and hyperparameters in the process.**Implementing eval_bireverse logic**\n\nI need to ensure our `create_network` function in `dsconv_pm.py` correctly passes the `eval_bireverse` parameter to `DSConvPMNet`. If both conditions for determinism and bi-reverse hold true, I'll compute averages for the features.\n\nTo reuse weights effectively, I'll define the backbone\
  \ only once. This way, I can call it with both the original and reversed inputs without duplicating parameters. I'll also make adjustments to the `hidden_dim` and optimizer settings to enhance performance. Finally, I'm crafting the submission file while ensuring compliance with training system requirements and writing necessary updates to the model file.**Preparing for submission**\n\nI’m considering logging the improved evaluations, but it might not be necessary. There’s a potential “cooldown period” for submissions, so since we've just completed 922, I’ll try to submit the new evaluation, 923.\n\nI need to prepare a YAML title for the submission and outline the necessary actions. I plan to update `dsconv_pm.py` to include `eval_bireverse` and `residual_scale` parameters. I'll ensure these parameters are passed correctly in the code. Finally, I’ll set up the hyperparameters and call `create_optimizer`. Let’s get to it!"
