- text: "v3 improved overall (0.6134). Ablation 925 shows eval-time bi-reverse averaging is beneficial (notably PRS +0.12, MRL +0.03 vs no-avg). Next, isolate residual scaling impact while 929 (train-reverse OFF) runs.\n\n`/execute_action{review 929}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: DSConv-PM v3 with residual_scale=1.0\"\ntags: \"conv, dsconv, pooling, regularization, ablation, optimizer-adamw\"\nabstract: \"Tests whether mild residual scaling (α=0.5) contributes to gains; reset residual_scale to 1.0 while keeping train reverse augmentation and bi-reverse evaluation ON (v3 otherwise unchanged).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_pm_ablate_residual1 import _define_hyperparameters, create_optimizer, complete\n  from dsconv_pm_ablate_residual1 import create_network as create_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating model architecture**\n\nI think the core architecture should handle all sequence-level tasks, while the final layers can tailor predictions. However, let's steer clear of dataset-specific logic. Implementing a reverse-equivariant architecture might work generically, like pooling forward and reverse features across tasks. We already average evaluations and could add symmetric pooling to support invariance without dataset conditions. But if CRI-Off's labels lack reverse symmetry, enforcing invariance could harm performance.**Generalizing evaluation architecture**\n\nSince the evaluation involves multiple tasks, I want to focus on an architecture that generalizes well. We can add an optional invariance parameter, but I should be cautious of making it dataset-specific. Instead, I can set the default to a \"learn to fuse directions\" approach using a gating mechanism. This will weigh both forward and reverse features in a dataset-agnostic way. While keeping the\
  \ code minimal is essential, I could consider dual-direction processing for future versions, ensuring efficiency without overcomplicating.**Proposing changes for training**\n\nI’m considering dropping reverse augmentation for training while keeping eval-time averaging. I’ll have 929 test this setup to see if it improves CRI-Off and classification accuracy without heavily impacting PRS/APA. I should also review the training code, especially how dropout RNG is applied, which is currently used correctly. Adding batch normalization might not be advisable in JAX with small steps, so LayerNorm could be a better option. Drafting a Private Memory capsule could help capture insights, which feels beneficial, while maintaining awareness of our token budget since we're at 20% usage.**Preparing to execute actions**\n\nThe user is looking for specific actions, focusing on parsing /execute_action commands and YAML blocks. I need to check evaluation statuses and submit the residual1 ablation when concurrency\
  \ allows it. I've noted that 925 has finished, and currently, only 929 is running, so I can submit one more. However, based on the latest room output at tick 593, it seems 925 is done, and we can move forward with the submission. I should also consider reducing the tag list to a maximum of six and define them appropriately.**Planning next actions**\n\nI’m ready to proceed with a few important actions. First, I'll review the status of 929, which is still pending. Next, I will submit the residual1 ablation as the concurrency allows for one more submission. There’s also a possibility of setting a rank for authors, but I may skip that. I'll ensure my submission title is correctly quoted due to the colon. Lastly, I should minimize token usage by not writing unnecessary new files. \n\nHere are the commands I'm planning:\n\n1) /execute_action{review 929}  \n2) /execute_action{submit 1} with ablate_residual1.  \n3) Optionally set rank author or filter, but I think I’ll skip that."
