tick: 594
role: model
parts:
- text: "Ablations show:\n- Eval-time bi-reverse averaging helps overall (notably PRS, MRL), but harms CRI-Off.\n- Training-time reverse augmentation is crucial for APA/MRL/PRS but appears harmful for ncRNA.\n\nI’ll submit v4 with dataset-aware reverse handling:\n- CRI-Off: disable both training reverse and eval bi-reverse.\n- ncRNA: disable training reverse, keep eval bi-reverse.\n- Others (APA, PRS, MRL, Modif, CRI-On): keep both ON.\nCore architecture unchanged.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_pm_v4.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/models')\n  from dsconv_pm import create_network as base_create_network\n  from dsconv_pm import create_optimizer as base_create_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Base v3 settings\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\": 1e-3,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 224,\n          \"num_blocks\": 6,\n          \"kernel_size\": 9,\n    \
    \      \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"pos_dropout_rate\": 0.07,\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"powermean_init\": 2.0,\n          \"enable_reverse_aug\": True,\n          \"eval_bireverse\": True,\n          \"residual_scale\": 0.5,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      # Dataset-aware reverse handling\n      h = dict(hparams)\n      ds = h.get(\"dataset\")\n      if ds == \"CRI-Off\":\n          # Orientation matters: disable reverse both train/eval\n          h[\"enable_reverse_aug\"] = False\n          h[\"eval_bireverse\"] = False\n      elif ds == \"ncRNA\":\n          # Training reverse hurt; keep eval averaging\n          h[\"enable_reverse_aug\"] = False\n          h[\"eval_bireverse\"] = True\n      else:\n          # Keep both ON for APA, PRS, MRL, Modif, CRI-On\n          h[\"enable_reverse_aug\"] = True\n          h[\"eval_bireverse\"] = True\n      return base_create_network(h)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3):\n    \
    \  # Same optimizer as v3\n      return base_create_optimizer(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.012, clip=0.75, b1=0.85, b2=0.99)\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get(\"dataset\")\n      val = trial_data.get(\"val_metric\")\n      seed = trial_data.get(\"seed\")\n      print(f\"[Complete] v4 {ds} val_metric={val} seed={seed}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv-PM v4 — Dataset-aware reverse handling (disable on CRI-Off; train-off on ncRNA)\"\ntags: \"conv, dsconv, pooling, reverse-invariance, regularization, optimizer-adamw\"\nabstract: \"Same compact DSConv + power-mean model, but toggles reverse augmentation/bi-reverse evaluation per dataset: OFF for CRI-Off (orientation-sensitive), train-reverse OFF but eval averaging ON for ncRNA; both ON for APA/PRS/MRL/Modif/CRI-On.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_pm_v4 import _define_hyperparameters, create_optimizer,\
    \ complete\n  from dsconv_pm_v4 import create_network as create_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating classification performance**\n\nI’m considering how capacity affects classification accuracy. If performance degrades, it might be due to overfitting or underfitting. But it's curious how dropout or positional dropout could impact classifying 100-length sequences, which doesn’t seem excessively long. I also wonder if a \"residual_scale\" of 0.5 could be hindering classification. It'll definitely be interesting to see the results starting from 930; I feel like there’s a lot to unpack here!**Proposing v4 updates**\n\nI’m suggesting some adjustments for v4: keeping the hidden_dim at 224 and the number of blocks around 5 or 6. I want to maintain power-mean pooling and introduce dataset-specific toggles. For instance, turning on `enable_reverse_aug` for certain datasets and off for others like ncRNA. The results from 924 indicate reverse evaluation helped ncRNA, but it’s still lower than v2. I’m keen to see how changes in residual_scale affect stability and classification\
  \ before making further adjustments. Also, I’ll prepare code changes and a new submission file in the meantime!**Considering improvements for head dropout**\n\nI'm thinking about another potential improvement: making \"head dropout\" adjustable. I’ve used a dropout rate for the head and might try setting it to 0.05 based on Noema II, which could enhance classification metrics. However, I want to keep changes minimal. I also consider integrating \"Z-normalization at capture.\" While it might not be critical to the network itself, adding 'LayerNorm' and \"channel gating\" could help with calibration. For CRI-Off, I’ll focus on dataset-specific toggles to disable reverse training and evaluation. Maybe I'll adjust the conv 'kernel_size' from 9 to 7 due to the shorter sequence.**Setting up dataset-specific toggles**\n\nI'm considering that the core architecture can remain largely the same across datasets, but we can customize the final layers for each prediction type. This indicates that changes\
  \ like gating might be permissible, but it's safer to only use toggles instead of altering kernel size. I plan to build our stored module to accept these dataset-specific toggles, leveraging the hparams dictionary. I'll implement this in the wrapper to override hparams flags based on the dataset, keeping in mind that the current code just returns the model using the given hparams.**Preparing new submission dsconv_pm_v4.py**\n\nI’m planning to write a new submission named 'dsconv_pm_v4.py' to set up dataset-specific toggles. In the `create_network` function, I'm checking the dataset and adjusting flags like `enable_reverse_aug` for training and `eval_bireverse` for evaluation purposes. I want to maintain residual_scale at 0.5 for now but may finalize it once the results from 930 come in. I'll ensure the module accepts dataset-specific toggles while carefully modifying hparams before calling `dsconv_pm.create_network`. It's crucial to avoid excessive dataset-specific tuning while preparing\
  \ the code!**Finalizing submission actions**\n\nI’m considering setting `page_size` to 5 to manage token size, which is fine. Now, the final message should summarize the necessary actions with minimal commentary. I’ll include bullet points while ensuring we use `/execute_action` commands. Here’s what I have:\n\n- Insights: Train reverse augmentation is vital for APA/MRL; eval-time averaging is beneficial, but harmful on CRI-Off.\n  \n- Actions: Write `v4` to storage and submit `v4`. \n\nI’ll prepare the YAML and ensure we cover the specified title without special characters. Now let’s finalize the code and package everything together!"
