role: model
parts:
- text: "v5/v5g show strong long-range gains (APA/MRL/Modif), but CRI-Off benefits from disabling reverse, and ncRNA may benefit from attention pooling. I’ll combine dilated DSConv + attention pooling with dataset-aware reverse toggles (OFF for CRI-Off; ON for others) and submit.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_pm_v7_dil_attn_da.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/models')\n  from dsconv_pm_dil_attn import create_network as base_create_network\n  from dsconv_pm_dil_attn import create_optimizer as base_create_optimizer\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Dilated DSConv + attention pooling for classification; dataset-aware reverse\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\": 1e-3,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 224,\n          \"kernel_size\": 9,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"pos_dropout_rate\": 0.07,\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"powermean_init\"\
    : 2.0,\n          \"residual_scale\": 0.5,\n          \"attn_hidden\": 224,\n          \"ds_dilations\": [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 16],\n          # defaults; per-dataset toggles set in create_network\n          \"enable_reverse_aug\": True,\n          \"eval_bireverse\": True,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      h = dict(hparams)\n      ds = h.get(\"dataset\")\n      # CRI-Off is orientation-sensitive: disable both reverse aug and bi-reverse eval\n      if ds == \"CRI-Off\":\n          h[\"enable_reverse_aug\"] = False\n          h[\"eval_bireverse\"] = False\n      else:\n          h[\"enable_reverse_aug\"] = True\n          h[\"eval_bireverse\"] = True\n      h[\"num_blocks\"] = len(h.get(\"ds_dilations\", [1]))\n      return base_create_network(h)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3):\n      # Use slightly lower wd per prior gains\n      return base_create_optimizer(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.0095, clip=0.75, b1=0.85, b2=0.99)\n\n  def complete(params,\
    \ opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get(\"dataset\")\n      val = trial_data.get(\"val_metric\")\n      seed = trial_data.get(\"seed\")\n      print(f\"[Complete] v7-dil-attn-da {ds} val_metric={val} seed={seed}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv-PM v7 — Dilated + attention pooling with dataset-aware reverse (CRI-Off OFF)\"\ntags: \"conv, dsconv, dilation, pooling, attention, augmentation\"\nabstract: \"Combines a dilated depthwise-separable conv backbone with power-mean pooling and a lightweight attention pooling head (for classification). Reverse augmentation and bi-reverse eval are disabled only for CRI-Off; enabled elsewhere.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_pm_v7_dil_attn_da import _define_hyperparameters, create_optimizer, complete\n  from dsconv_pm_v7_dil_attn_da import create_network as create_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating dataset-aware impacts**\n\nI’m thinking about how reverse augmentation may be harmful. Earlier, I noticed that dataset-aware toggling improved CRI-Off from 0.0399 to 0.065. Clearly, dataset-aware reverse is beneficial for CRI-Off, but while v5 had a higher PRS, it had lower ncRNA compared to v5g. This suggests that a hybrid approach using dataset-aware reverse for CRI-Off while keeping it enabled for other metrics could work well. Plus, v6 might boost classification metrics even further, especially for long-range tasks.**Planning v7 architecture**\n\nI'm thinking about creating v7 with a dilated DSConv backbone and incorporating dataset-aware reverse. I'd disable reverse specifically for CRI-Off while enabling it for other metrics. Adding attention pooling could help with classification tasks too. I'd adjust optimizer parameters and consider implementing learned per-dataset head dropout, but keep the core architecture consistent across datasets. Customizing\
  \ final layers for task types should be fine. I see potential in experimenting with power-mean p initialization, and we need to manage current processing too.**Creating v7 architecture**\n\nI believe there's a limit of 2 concurrent pending evaluations, since we got an error last time when trying to submit a third. Right now, we have 939 jobs running, and I see that v5/v5g and several others have finished, leaving one pending. It seems we can submit one more! \n\nI plan to prepare a wrapper file for v7 that combines dataset-aware reverse and attention pooling, adjusting hyperparameters as needed. We'll set the dataset-aware toggles appropriately for CRI-Off and keep reverse on for ncRNA this time. Then we can label it as \"v7 dataset-aware attention.\"**Improving PRS and CRI-On**\n\nI'm reflecting on the differences between v5 and v5g, where v5 had a PRS of 0.5296 and v5g had 0.4791. I'm hopeful that using dataset-aware toggles will help improve PRS while keeping ncRNA high thanks to attention\
  \ pooling. \n\nWhile considering minor optimizer modifications, it seems b1 was used consistently at 0.85, which I think we should keep for now. Although CRI-On is around 0.43-0.45, I wonder if attention pooling could aid small dataset CRI-On despite it being tied to classification. I’m thinking about attempting a weighted mean aggregator or keeping things simple with a residual channel aggregator for now. The next step is to implement the v7 wrapper and submit it!**Finalizing v7 submission**\n\nI'm considering using Research Counter filter tags to check other agents' code, just to make sure we're not duplicating state-of-the-art methods while still being independent. I need to implement the storage write for our v7 file and submit it since we currently have one running evaluation.\n\nI also want to maintain a tag limit of six in the YAML and rank scores, though it’s not essential. I'll ensure we quote any special characters in titles and check for the necessary Flax layer configurations.\
  \ Lastly, we'll set the dataset-aware toggles accordingly before crafting our commands for submission."
