parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{review 945}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noema/submissions/dsconv_pm_v7b_dil_attn_da_do.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/models')\n  from dsconv_pm_dil_attn import create_network as base_create_network\n  import optax\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Base defaults; dataset-specific tweaks applied in create_network\n      return {\n          \"learning_rate\": 1e-3,\n          \"hidden_dim\": 224,\n          \"kernel_size\": 9,\n          \"dropout_rate\": 0.10,\n          \"pos_dropout_rate\": 0.07,\n          \"head_hidden\": 128,\n          \"powermean_init\": 2.0,\n          \"residual_scale\": 0.5,\n          \"attn_hidden\": 224,\n          \"ds_dilations\": [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 16],\n          \"enable_reverse_aug\": True,\n          \"eval_bireverse\": True,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      # Dataset-aware reverse and regularization schedule\n  \
    \    h = dict(hparams)\n      ds = h.get(\"dataset\")\n\n      # Reverse toggles\n      if ds == \"CRI-Off\":\n          h[\"enable_reverse_aug\"] = False\n          h[\"eval_bireverse\"] = False\n      else:\n          h[\"enable_reverse_aug\"] = True\n          h[\"eval_bireverse\"] = True\n\n      # Regularization schedule\n      if ds in (\"Modif\", \"ncRNA\"):          # classification tasks\n          h[\"dropout_rate\"] = 0.12\n          h[\"pos_dropout_rate\"] = 0.08\n          h[\"attn_hidden\"] = h.get(\"attn_hidden\", 224)\n      else:                                  # regression tasks (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, PRS, MRL)\n          h[\"dropout_rate\"] = 0.08\n          h[\"pos_dropout_rate\"] = 0.05\n          h[\"attn_hidden\"] = h.get(\"attn_hidden\", 224)  # unused for regression\n\n      h[\"num_blocks\"] = len(h.get(\"ds_dilations\", [1]))\n      return base_create_network(h)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 1e-3):\n      # Stronger regularization & momentum\
    \ to stabilize regression (APA/MRL/PRS)\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(0.75),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, b1=0.9, b2=0.999, weight_decay=0.012)\n      )\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      ds = trial_data.get(\"dataset\")\n      val = trial_data.get(\"val_metric\")\n      seed = trial_data.get(\"seed\")\n      print(f\"[Complete] v7b-da-do {ds} val_metric={val} seed={seed}\")\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv-PM v7b — Dilated + attention pooling, dataset-aware reverse and dropout, AdamW(wd=0.012,0.9,0.999)\"\ntags: \"conv, dsconv, dilation, attention, regularization, optimizer-adamw\"\nabstract: \"Builds on v7: dilated DSConv backbone with power-mean + attention pooling; disables reverse only for CRI-Off; adds dataset-aware dropout (higher for classification, lower for regression); stronger AdamW (wd=0.012, b1=0.9,b2=0.999) to stabilize regression tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  import\
    \ sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noema/submissions')\n  from dsconv_pm_v7b_dil_attn_da_do import _define_hyperparameters, create_optimizer, complete\n  from dsconv_pm_v7b_dil_attn_da_do import create_network as create_network\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating training components**\n\nI’m considering which components are important based on the ablation results. Keeping training with reverse ON seems beneficial, especially since turning it off drastically harmed APA. It looks like eval-time bi-reverse off hurt PRS and MRL, so keeping it ON is better. The residual scale also matters; there’s a decent difference between 0.5 and 1.0. I noticed that dilated convolutions improved APA, MRL, and modifications significantly, while attention pooling boosted ncRNA and PRS notably, which is great!**Analyzing dataset-aware benefits**\n\nI'm noting that dataset-aware seems to help with CRI-Off, especially since v7 improved overall to 0.6568, which is the highest so far. However, I see that v7's CRI-On Spearman dropped to 0.4243 compared to v6's 0.4476, possibly due to noise from smaller sample sizes or weight decay variations. I've proposed some small modifications while we have one evaluation running with v6r. \n\nIt looks like\
  \ MRL decreased with v7, so I’m questioning if attention pooling might be affecting regression by changing head hidden dimensions.**Exploring weight decay and performance**\n\nI’m considering the potential impact of weight decay differences; for instance, v6 used 0.0095 while v5 had 0.012. Maybe a higher weight decay could help MRL, as v6r employs 0.012. The PRS improved impressively from v5's 0.5296 to v7's 0.5593, which is great to see! \n\nThere’s also a slight dip in ncRNA scores, possibly due to dataset-aware reverse influences. I’m thinking about trying \"pre-logit normalization\" techniques or simple \"z-norm\" on pooled features to maintain a unified scale while ensuring we keep things straightforward.**Considering gating and adjustments**\n\nI'm thinking about using gating to combine mean, max, and pmean contributions with a parameterized vector for dataset-specific weighting. The training code includes the dataset in hyperparameters, but the gating module isn't dataset-specific.\
  \ I could add a \"Featurewise Affine\" calibrator to model outputs. \n\nFor PRS in multi-label regression, we might adjust the head with output scaling without changing the loss—still using MSE. In the convolutional architecture, integrating \"Separable Squeeze-and-Excitation\" could adapt channel weights, and refining the dilation schedule while considering anti-aliasing would help avoid complications.**Planning improvements for v7b**\n\nNext, I'm looking at how to improve MRL in the \"v7b\" attempt by exploring weight decay and timeouts. I can't change timeouts, but I can adjust dropout rates—using lower rates for regression tasks and higher ones for classification. The training system allows dataset-specific settings, which helps maintain a similar architecture across datasets.\n\nI'm proposing \"v7h\" to implement dataset-aware dropout rates and consider experimenting with dropout rates for small datasets to enhance generalization. Ultimately, I need to analyze the scoreboard to focus\
  \ on MRL and CRI-On improvements while also considering run variability.**Proposing enhancements for v7b**\n\nIt looks like v6 performs better for CRI-On because it uses global reverse settings. I want to propose a variant, \"v7,\" that maintains these settings for CRI-On while potentially lowering the dropout rate to 0.08 for better regularization.\n\nFor MRL, I should include ds_dilations like 32 but be careful of aliasing, possibly smoothing convolutions. I’m considering a new optimizer that could allow for dataset-specific adjustments but it’s complex. My plan includes reviewing results from the previous runs, decreasing token usage, and submitting \"v7b\" with adjusted dropout rates for various tasks while keeping weight decay consistent.**Implementing network settings**\n\nI need to make sure that h['num_blocks'] is set to the length of h['ds_dilations']. Afterward, I'll return base_create_network(h) and implement create_optimizer using optax.adamw with parameters: weight_decay at\
  \ 0.012, b1 set to 0.9, and b2 to 0.999 with a clipping value of 0.75. Once that's done, I’ll submit with an appropriate title and set the necessary tags. I'll keep my comments minimal. Time to move forward!"
